#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231870} :activity/id #uuid "cb18bc73-8ca3-58d6-8195-4f448e87dc8e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049818 :topic/id #uuid "162c4fe5-4ba7-543f-815e-0c537518fc9a" :topic/math-academy-id 6819} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232041 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we used $\\max$ and $\\min$ to talk about the largest and smallest *values* a function can take on a set.\n\nIn this lesson, we learn two closely related pieces of notation:\n\n- $\\max$ and $\\min$ return the best (largest/smallest) *function value*.\n\n- $\\arg\\max$ and $\\arg\\min$ return the *input value* (the \"argument\") where that best value occurs.\n\nFor example, let $S = \\{1,2,3\\}$ and suppose\n\n$$\nf(1)=4,\\qquad f(2)=7,\\qquad f(3)=6.\n$$\n\nThen the largest function value is $7,$ and it occurs when $x=2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{max}_{x\\in S}f(x) & =7, \\\\ \\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{x\\in S}f(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the smallest function value, and $\\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input where that smallest value occurs.\n\n**Watch out!** Sometimes the best value occurs at more than one input. In that case, we need to clarify what $\\arg\\max$ (or $\\arg\\min$) means. We will handle this after we practice the basic case." :tutorial/id #uuid "bba3a61c-b816-520d-909b-990b65f4c370" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15114 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f81d5a0b-4aa5-57a2-8cd4-4289eca1c411" :step/math-academy-id 629516 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231871}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231871 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231450 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231376 :question/id #uuid "6308dcce-94b4-57ff-9d03-a184ad2048bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-22126"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f56e312-a8c4-5c65-9893-3ebc38120b4d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049900}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534076}] :knowledge-point/title "Distinguishing Between Max/Min and Argmax/Argmin"} :step/id #uuid "2c4bb6ef-8180-592f-a235-299caf0aa949" :step/math-academy-id 629517 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231872}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231872 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232042 :tutorial/content "Sometimes a function attains its largest (or smallest) value at more than one input. In that case, there are multiple inputs that satisfy the definition of $\\arg\\max$ (or $\\arg\\min$).\n\nIn this course, when a tie occurs, we report the *set* of all inputs where the maximum is achieved as follows:\n\n$$\n\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x) = {x \\in S{:} f(x) = \\max_{u \\in S} f(u)}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when a tie occurs, we report the set of all inputs where the minimum is achieved as follows:\n\n$$\n\\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x) = {x \\in S{:} f(x) = \\min_{u \\in S} f(u)}.\n$$\n\nThe quantities $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ and $\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$ are still single numbers." :tutorial/id #uuid "8ef1fb19-42d3-5471-a53f-023f932c2ad2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15115 :tutorial/title "Argmax and Argmin With Ties"} :step/id #uuid "4d293468-053f-5f98-87b1-78b1633c6e5a" :step/math-academy-id 629520 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231873}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231873 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231451 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231377 :question/id #uuid "b8e4dcaf-38c1-5244-9bca-2e0eab32c34c" :question/math-academy-id "e-22127"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "258ff70f-5f41-5ea6-a0c2-bf74e7ce1cff" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534075}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Argmax and Argmin With Ties"} :step/id #uuid "316698db-832d-542a-826d-ce5cc6c0afb4" :step/math-academy-id 629521 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231874}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231874 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231452 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231378 :question/id #uuid "c901e488-7c50-5af5-85b2-ce70b1447a8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-22128"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24771a14-af10-5bc8-94d4-17c28b180c84" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534096}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing and Reading Formal Notation"} :step/id #uuid "8ba7e4ab-2bfb-5d57-8de9-e8f4b6255634" :step/math-academy-id 629524}] :activity/title "Argmax and Argmin Notation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231551}]}
